today marks the 40th anniversary of the  deadliest aviation accident in history  it happened on the small Spanish island  of Tenerife off the coast of West Africa  two jumbo jets one American collided on  March 27 1977 Jeff Glor spoke with a  survivor of the deadly crash and looks  at how the factors that caused it are  still relevant today  Jeff good morning Charlie good morning  to you the unusual events and errors  that led to this disaster almost too  hard to believe even 40 years later an  act of terror at a different Airport  miscommunication weather and a pilots  mistake 583 people died but 61 survived  some are still with us today  they are typically very reluctant to  speak unless they believe it can help  for decades ago Tenerife the picturesque  quiet Spanish Canary Islands suddenly  home to the worst airline accident the  world had ever seen both then and now  historically it's an accident with an  almost mythical kind of aura around it  Patrick Smith is a commercial airline  pilot and author who was studied  Tenerife extensively where that comes  from I think is partly the bizarre  cascading series of ironies and  coincidences that led to it happening  beginning with the fact that neither of  these two planes was supposed to be at  Tenerife in the first place shifted to  Tenerife by a global threat that still  haunts the industry the Pan Am and KLM  jumbo jets were diverted to Tenerife  yesterday after a terrorist bomb  explosion at the Canary Islands main  airport in Las Palmas two 747s ended up  on an infrequently used runway as both  planes waited for several hours an  unusually dense fog descended eventually  the tower sent a message to a KLM flight  to taxi to the end of the runway and  turn around then instructed a Pan Am  flight to follow the KLM jet got into  position and announced they were ready  to go Pan Am Pilots heard this became  startled click their mic to say hey  we're still on the runway because of  radio interference the KLM plane didn't  get that message  the pilot thinking he was cleared for  takeoff sent his plane rock  on the runway with a Pan Am plane was  still taxiing Bob Bragg the first  officer of the Pan Am flight spoke to  CBS News in 2000 as soon as I saw the  man moving and coming at us I started  saying get off as we were turning to the  left I looked back out of my side window  and that's when he had lifted off the  ground and become airborne right as  close to us as I could seem the KLM  plane wasn't high enough on contact it  burst into flames and tore the top of  the Pan Am jet clear off everyone aboard  the KLM flight was killed but inside the  Pan Am flight some remained alive you  haven't talked about this publicly in in  almost 40 years right I wonder what  you're thinking here as the anniversary  arrives how fortunate I am including  Joan feathers we found outside Phoenix  just last week sat there thinking this  is the end  so as we made our way to the side of the  plane all these little implosions of  fire I just felt that it was imminent I  did not want to burn up so there was a  doctor from Chicago that had been in the  plane and he was down below and he said  jump I'll catch you what did you see  when you looked around I could tell that  there was a lot of these little fires  that were getting bigger so I kept  running and it wasn't maybe 15 seconds  and the plane went up like an atom bomb  it just and then huge fire investigators  pinned most of the blame on the KLM  pilot Jacob Venzon who was considered  such an exceptional flier he was the  face of the company he was on KLM  billboards in KLM advertisements one of  the the famous little nuggets of the  story is that when word got back to KLM  that one of their planes had crashed  they went looking for captain Van Zandt  and to go to the scene to figure out  what happened unaware that he was  actually the captain the Tenerife  disaster the worst in aviation history  remains relevant today we've engineered  away what used to be the causes of some  of the worst crashes ever but this one I  think  the fundamental cause or causes are  really still there that means crowded  runways it means potential pilot error  what else  runway congestion air traffic control  communications issues things like that  those problems are still out there  there's not a crisis but we shouldn't be  complacent about that as for Joan  feathers she's never stopped thinking  about Tenerife every year on March 27th  I remember and it's as if it happened  not long ago do you think about it on  when you every time every time you flush  every time you fly as Patrick Smith  rightly points out flying today has  never been safer the improvements made  since the 70s and 80s have been vast and  measurable  that's a communication and congestion  issues still come up and she still gets  on a plane and flies she still gets on a  plane yeah she says sometimes she sits  next to what she calls a white knuckled  fly or somebody who's very nervous yeah  and she usually she doesn't tell them  the story typically but she says that if  you only knew  that's so heartbreaking though Jeff a  series of things could have prevented  that and yet that it happened on the  ground - is also very frightening what's  the most alarming thing that they have  not fixed well the the radio frequencies  I mean the communication issues that  come up when when they send messages  between tower and and pilots sometimes  if they happen at the same time they  don't hear that's one of the many issues  that happened back then 40 years ago  Jeff thank you sure
 

